   "source": [
    "### Comparison to baselines\n",
    "\n",
    "On the held-out test set the spectrogram + transfer-learning model reaches 0.382 balanced accuracy and an MCC of 0.367, against the dummy baselines' 0.20 balanced accuracy and an MCC of approximately zero, so the model has learned diagnostic structure from the log-spectrograms rather than predicting the dominating class. Raw accuracy (0.659) is close to the most_frequent baseline (~0.71), but that similarity is misleading: the dummy classifier reaches it by predicting myocardial infarction for every record, whereas this model produces correct predictions in four of the five classes, with recalls of 0.72 for myocardial infarction, 0.71 for healthy controls and 0.33 for dysrhythmia. The rare diagnoses remain the limiting factor and across repeated runs at both 100 and 1000 epochs balanced accuracy stayed within 0.377-0.390 while which rare class collapsed to zero, indicating the ceiling is set by sample size rather than by training length or by the choice of representation. The training curves confirm this: train loss falls to ~0.00 within the first few dozen epochs while validation loss rises above 4.8, so with 6-8 training records for three of the five diagnoses the network memorises those examples instead of generalising from them, which is also the most likely source of any gap to the waveform model trained during the lecture."
   ]
